From f26fa0e7f6bb49b8418311ed6a4191dd782ae8c1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObeseCatLord <6099645+ObeseCatLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 11:02:24 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 188/191] IOP: idle grants of EE cycles are counted, not run
 through the scheduler

The EE grants the IOP its cycles at every branch it takes (8 cycles per call/return, 32 per loop back edge), millions
of times a second. Each grant went through the call scope (two recursive-mutex locks), the subsystem, and one pass of
the IOP scheduler loop even when the IOP had nothing to do until a known later cycle. On a Steam Deck that chain was
about 30 % of the game thread in a movie and 15-20 % in a battle.

After a grant that is applied, the emulator computes how many EE cycles further grants may sum up to before one of
them can do more than move the clocks: until the earliest of every deadline it has (thread delay, DMA completion,
alarm/queued callback, hardware timer, the SPU's next sample), or until the EE clock has repaid the IOP's lead when
something is due now. Grants inside that horizon are summed (IopEmulator::tryDeferEeCycles, asked by
PS2Runtime::advanceIopEeCycles before it takes the call scope); the grant that reaches the horizon, and anything else
that enters the emulator, first turns the sum into what the grants would have been one by one: clock ticks.

- The horizon is void as soon as a thread changes state (IopKernel::stateEpoch): a reverse-RPC completion wakes its
  thread from the host, outside any emulator call (found by ps2_iop_reverse_rpc_tests).
- No horizon while the IOP is inside a call, a callback, an RPC wait or an SPU access, with an SPU IRQ pending, or
  with a lead beyond kMaxIopLeadCycles (the next grant forgives part of it: not additive).
- IopEmulator::cycles() counts the deferred grants.
- GA2_IOP_DEFER=0 turns it off. GA2_IOP_DEFER=check applies every deferrable grant the old way and aborts if it
  changed anything but the clocks (instructions run, thread/timer/DMA/callback queues, SPU position, interrupt
  status).

Game timing is unchanged: the same grant as before is the first to run IOP code, service a deadline or produce a
sample, at the same IOP cycle.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
